WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.050
So good morning everyone.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:17.660
It's 9:00 AM on the west coast, and I'm Alex
Bui, co-director of the BD2K Center's coordination

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:18.660
center.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:23.360
So last time in the series, we completed our
discussions on data modeling and inference.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.240
And today, we kind of shift gears to talk
about some other topics of interest in biomedical

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.470
big data.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:32.611
So the first topic we'll be covering is one
of growing interest-- open science.

00:00:32.611 --> 00:00:38.790
Questions around making science transparent
and reproducible and making it available has

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:39.910
important implications.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:46.170
Not just within the academic and scientific
communities, but to the broader public.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:49.150
And with us today is Dr. Brian Nosek, who
is a Professor of psychology at the University

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:50.150
of Virginia.

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:54.370
Doctor Nosek is co-founder and executive director
of the Center of Open Science, which advocates

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:57.540
and supports the practice of open science
on a global scale.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:03.740
In 2015, he was named on Nature's 10 List,
and he's also on the Chronicle of Higher Education's

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:04.740
influence list.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.240
Brian thank you so much for joining us this
morning.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.119
I'm looking forward to hearing more about
open science and your perspectives on how

00:01:11.119 --> 00:01:13.409
we as a community can enable it.

00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:16.380
And with that, the floor is yours.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.380
Thanks Alex.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:21.320
I'm very happy to be speaking with this group
today.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:28.920
The term open science is an unusual term,
because it is essentially redundant, right?

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.400
Scientific claims don't become credible based
on authority, or insistence that they're true,

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:34.640
or logical argument.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.760
They become credible based on the availability
of the evidence that support the scientific

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:40.760
claim.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:45.680
So that includes the outcome of the report
of what the person-- it's how they summarize,

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:48.920
how they did their research and arrived at
those claims.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:53.930
It includes the materials and methodology
that was the basis for generating the evidence

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:57.200
or applying analytics to the data that are
generated.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.690
And the data itself that are the content that
supports that claim.

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:10.379
So the fact that we have a discussion and
an unusual term about open science isn't suggesting

00:02:10.379 --> 00:02:17.290
something about a different way of doing science,
but rather the fact that the values of openness

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:21.700
in science are not consistent, necessarily,
with the daily practice of how science is

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:22.700
operating today.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:29.400
So really, the open science movement is about,
I think, values in addressing the gap between

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:35.080
daily practice and core principles of how
science is presumed to operate.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:40.010
So one way to start is to just to review what
are those core principles of what makes science

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:43.690
science-- as a way of knowing, as a way of
learn about the world.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:48.610
And Robert Merton talks about these in the
1940s and 50s, when he was trying to characterize

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.220
the norms of science or the values of science.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:55.300
So one, he identified communality-- the open
sharing of information.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:59.830
Claims become credible based on the sharing
of the evidence supporting those claims, versus

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:00.830
the counter-norm-- secrecy.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.080
Nope, just got to trust me.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:04.700
I'm a scientist, I know what I'm talking
about.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.290
Just believe me.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.090
The second-- universalism.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.650
Research is evaluated based on its own merit,
right?

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:15.540
The evidence itself is the basis of deciding
whether the claims are credible or not.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.191
Versus particularism-- evaluating research
based on the authority, the prestige, the

00:03:19.191 --> 00:03:22.510
eminence of the person making the claim.

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:26.020
A third that Merton identified was disinterestedness.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:31.030
Researchers are motivated by knowledge and
discovery, versus the counter-norm of self-interestedness--

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:34.410
just trying to get ahead of the person down
the hall.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:40.750
Likewise, researchers are-- consider all new
evidence, even against prior work.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:44.550
Organized skepticism versus the counternorm
of organized dogmatism.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:50.370
I spend my career finding the initial results
and then fighting off all the attackers in

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:55.050
the field to defend my particular point of
view, my theory, my findings.

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:58.530
And while Merton didn't talk about it, a lot
of philosophers and sociologists of science

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:03.770
have talked about a norm of quality as compared
to the counter-norm of quantity.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:10.030
Now those may be easily recognizable values
and norms of how science is perceived, but

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:12.680
a question is do actually researchers endorse
those norms?

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:18.459
Do they buy in to these as the values of science,
compared to those counter-norms that they

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:20.600
could buy into.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:24.780
Anderson and her colleagues get a large-scale
survey of NIH ORDs.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:30.030
Something like 3,300 people were in their
sample to see whether they endorsed the counter-norms

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:31.800
or the norms.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:36.800
And they-- this graph here shows early career
which means people that were in a post-doctoral

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:37.800
award.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:43.560
Mid-career were folks that were in their first
R01 awards, so average age around 40.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:48.120
And what I'm showing you here is a cumulative
plot of the responses to that survey, where

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:53.720
the grey indicates people who endorse the
norms over the counter-norms on average.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:56.919
Whereas black are those who endorse the counter-norms
over the norms on average.

00:04:56.919 --> 00:05:04.160
So what you're seeing is 90% or so of the
participants in this survey said that they

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.500
endorse those norms of science over the counter-norms.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:10.639
And the gray hatched lines are people that
said, well, I sort of endorse both of these

00:05:10.639 --> 00:05:11.639
equally.

00:05:11.639 --> 00:05:13.880
So there's broad endorsement for the norms
of science.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.919
But then Anderson and her colleagues said
OK, that's fine.

00:05:16.919 --> 00:05:18.090
Don't tell me what you endorse.

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:21.810
Tell me how you behave in your everyday research.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:23.130
And it looks like this.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:28.310
So mostly, still more than 60% of early and
mid-career researchers say that they behave

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:30.380
according to the norms of science.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:35.160
But many are acknowledging that those counter-norms
of competition, of trying to get ahead, of

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:42.320
keeping information themselves, of dogmatic
thinking are driving their behavior to some

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:43.320
degree.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:47.620
And still most people do not endorse-- say
that they don't behave according to counter-claims

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:48.880
over the norms.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:49.990
Then they said, OK, OK.

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:54.970
Don't tell me what you do as an individual,
tell me about the people in your area of research.

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:56.630
How did they behave?

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:59.750
And the distribution of responses looks like
this.

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:04.949
So most people perceive that
their community-- the culture around them--

00:06:04.949 --> 00:06:10.669
behaves more according to the counter-norms
rather than the norms of science.

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:17.199
And this is a challenging situation, because
the gap between what my values are-- those

00:06:17.199 --> 00:06:23.030
first graphs saying I want to be open, I want
to be reproducible work, I want to be skeptical

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:24.430
about my own work.

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:32.330
Versus what I perceive is reinforced, is incentivized,
is the dominant behavior in my culture-- creates

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:36.080
a gap between how I think science should be
done and how I think it is being done and

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:39.949
what it takes to be successful in the field.

00:06:39.949 --> 00:06:41.259
And that's a significant challenge.

00:06:41.259 --> 00:06:45.930
And part of what is characterized as the reproducibility
crisis or whatever want to-- term you want

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:50.979
to use that's been in discussion in the last
few years-- is a lot of different kinds of

00:06:50.979 --> 00:06:56.620
behaviors that may be good for advancing one's
own career-- making one's research more publishable--

00:06:56.620 --> 00:07:02.169
but not necessarily good for the accuracy
or the reproducibility of the evidence.

00:07:02.169 --> 00:07:07.889
And there are lots and lots of research and
discussions about the various areas of limitation

00:07:07.889 --> 00:07:11.360
in the quality or rigor or reproducibility
of work.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:15.449
But we can characterize some of the main ones
like this, right?

00:07:15.449 --> 00:07:16.449
Low power.

00:07:16.449 --> 00:07:21.550
Most of the investigations of the power of
research design suggests that we don't even

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:25.889
have enough power to detect the effects that
we are detecting, increasing both the likelihood

00:07:25.889 --> 00:07:30.910
of Type I errors-- false positives-- and Type
II errors-- false negatives.

00:07:30.910 --> 00:07:36.289
Researchers have remarkable flexibility in
the kinds of analyses that they do of the

00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:41.169
same data and then can select among those
which are the analyses that are most favorable

00:07:41.169 --> 00:07:45.640
to their interests, whether that interest
is publication or their particular point of

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:47.210
view, or whatever else.

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:51.470
I do many experiments in my lab and only a
subset of them get into papers.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:54.060
So what is that process of selective reporting?

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:59.819
What ends up getting filtered out, and is
that a biasing influence over the reliability

00:07:59.819 --> 00:08:02.550
of the work that's end up produced?

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:09.330
No results are considered less interesting,
even less interpretable, than positive results-leading

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:13.960
to them being more likely to be ignored, or
not published, or not even written up for

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:16.319
potential publication.

00:08:16.319 --> 00:08:21.259
And the main driver of scientific success
is innovation.

00:08:21.259 --> 00:08:22.919
Finding out new possibilities.

00:08:22.919 --> 00:08:24.210
What things could be true.

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:30.900
Pushing new areas of interest that motivate
new theoretical domains or new possibilities.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:33.560
And there are good incentives for replication.

00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:36.430
For verifying findings that have been proposed.

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:41.310
For increasing the reliability of the estimates--
of effects that have been observed.

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:46.480
And so as a consequence, there's lots and
lots of innovation, but without the practices

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:53.079
of verification, of skepticism, of questioning,
of extending in incremental but cumulative

00:08:53.079 --> 00:08:54.600
ways.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:59.300
Now these are problems that have been well
discussed in the literature, and not just

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:00.879
in the last five or six years.

00:09:00.879 --> 00:09:06.910
The references that I have below are just
a sample of the many methodologists' discussions

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:12.100
about these very issues that occurred in the
1950s, 60s, and 70s.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:16.850
And all of the solutions that are being discussed
now for how we can increase reproducibly are

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:22.569
the same kinds of solutions that were identified
during that time period, suggesting that we

00:09:22.569 --> 00:09:25.269
understand what the problem is and what the
solutions are.

00:09:25.269 --> 00:09:29.850
We just haven't developed effective
mechanisms for addressing it.

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:36.000
And so at the Center for Open Science, we
exist as a nonprofit technology company that

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.149
has a mission to increase openness, integrity,
reproducibility of research.

00:09:41.149 --> 00:09:46.050
And I want to just-- during this presentation
today-- give you an overview of some of the

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:51.850
activities that we have for trying to nudge
the practices of science to be more aligned

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:54.720
with those core values.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:10:00.839
So the-- sort of the center of what we think
is the key challenge for increasing openness

00:10:00.839 --> 00:10:05.600
and reproducibility in research is that the
incentives for my success as a practicing

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:09.800
researcher are focused on me getting it published,
not getting it right.

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:16.110
So the mechanisms of reward-- my likelihood
of getting my job, of getting tenure, of getting

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:21.879
advanced in my career depend-- at least the
easy way to characterize it-- is depend on

00:10:21.879 --> 00:10:27.449
me publishing as frequently as possible in
most prestigious outlets that I can.

00:10:27.449 --> 00:10:29.379
And not everything gets published.

00:10:29.379 --> 00:10:33.600
So positive results are more likely to get
published than negative results.

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:38.230
Novel results are more likely to be published
than replications or redundant results.

00:10:38.230 --> 00:10:42.670
And clean and tidy stories, where everything
fits together in a nice sequence are more

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:48.040
likely to get published than ones that have
lots of exceptions, lots of things that are

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:49.040
uncertain.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:50.749
Positive, novel, clean results are a goldmine.

00:10:50.749 --> 00:10:55.950
Those are the those are the golden eggs that
we might shoot for, because those are more

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:57.519
important, better results.

00:10:57.519 --> 00:11:01.610
But of course, in few laboratories does that
happen very often, right?

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:05.579
We don't get positive, novel, clean and tidy
results very easily because we're studying

00:11:05.579 --> 00:11:09.449
problems that we don't understand-- that's
why we're studying them.

00:11:09.449 --> 00:11:11.360
And progress is healthy.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:12.360
We get lots of exceptions.

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:13.779
Lots of things that don't make sense.

00:11:13.779 --> 00:11:15.239
Many things that don't fit together.

00:11:15.239 --> 00:11:17.180
Lots of false starts.

00:11:17.180 --> 00:11:19.839
And that's just the process of doing research.

00:11:19.839 --> 00:11:25.399
But all the incentives are pointing us to
turning that goop that's happening in the

00:11:25.399 --> 00:11:32.649
lab into beautiful stories at the end that
show a clean and tidy sequence that may be

00:11:32.649 --> 00:11:35.379
more beautiful than the evidence actually
supports.

00:11:35.379 --> 00:11:40.449
And that's where a lot of the key challenges
are-- is that the published literature may

00:11:40.449 --> 00:11:46.170
appear to be more reliable, more complete,
more credible than the evidence supporting

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:47.589
it actually is.

00:11:47.589 --> 00:11:52.509
Because we don't actually see all of the evidence
that has been generated and is being used

00:11:52.509 --> 00:11:56.720
to make the claims that appear in the literature.

00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:01.779
So there are a lot of barriers to try and
to address and make all of that research process

00:12:01.779 --> 00:12:06.124
more transparent so that it can be evaluated
by the research community, for the research

00:12:06.124 --> 00:12:12.519
community to be able to do what the promise
of research is-- which is to be self-correcting.

00:12:12.519 --> 00:12:17.019
We cannot self-correct if we don't know the
basis of evidence for the claims.

00:12:17.019 --> 00:12:19.329
We can only self-correct based on what we
can see.

00:12:19.329 --> 00:12:25.589
And of course, the papers that come out of
the research process from my lab-- David [? Donoho

00:12:25.589 --> 00:12:28.769
?] at Stanford
characterized as advertising for the research,

00:12:28.769 --> 00:12:30.429
not research itself.

00:12:30.429 --> 00:12:36.209
It's my best description of what I think is
important about the work that we do that makes

00:12:36.209 --> 00:12:41.160
it as publishable as possible and also it's
a clear and tidy and interesting to the reader.

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:45.670
But of course, the things behind it are where
the real research are-- the materials, the

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:48.040
methodology, the protocols, the data.

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:52.269
All of that work that is what generated research--
that's the real research itself.

00:12:52.269 --> 00:12:57.949
So how can we make more of that accessible
so that the research community can be self-correcting?

00:12:57.949 --> 00:13:00.620
Well there's a number of important barriers.

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:04.759
One of them are those perceived norms that
we just talked about on the initial slide,

00:13:04.759 --> 00:13:11.319
where I see the culture being one of secrecy,
of people are advancing their careers, of

00:13:11.319 --> 00:13:13.869
being dogmatic in their approach.

00:13:13.869 --> 00:13:18.899
And I don't see myself wanting to do that,
but I have-- I'm confronted with a choice.

00:13:18.899 --> 00:13:24.549
I can either stay true to the values that
I have, of wanting to do science as it's supposed

00:13:24.549 --> 00:13:25.549
to be done.

00:13:25.549 --> 00:13:29.589
But at the cost of career advancement, because
I see that there are other practices that

00:13:29.589 --> 00:13:32.309
are accelerating people's careers.

00:13:32.309 --> 00:13:38.709
Or I could give in and sacrifice some of those
values and be able to advance my career by

00:13:38.709 --> 00:13:41.970
doing some of the behaviors that are more--
that are better for career advancement even

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:43.589
though they might not be better for science.

00:13:43.589 --> 00:13:46.470
And that's a difficult situation for anyone
to be in.

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:52.399
A second barrier is that we have these amazing
frontal lobes that give us lots of flexibility

00:13:52.399 --> 00:13:53.749
and reasoning.

00:13:53.749 --> 00:13:58.389
Where when we're confronted by multiple ways
we could analyze the data-- some of them look

00:13:58.389 --> 00:14:00.819
better for publication than others.

00:14:00.819 --> 00:14:04.259
Multiple studies that we could use to make--
be the basis of our claims-- some of which

00:14:04.259 --> 00:14:10.689
look better for publication or for our existing
theories than others, that we are likely to

00:14:10.689 --> 00:14:13.660
employ our reasoning.

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:18.720
Not as reasoning to arrive at a conclusion,
but as rationalization to justify a conclusion

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:20.619
that we already have.

00:14:20.619 --> 00:14:26.180
And that is a lion's share of how reasoning
actually works-- is that we have existing

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:31.779
beliefs and we look for information that helps
to support our existing beliefs, our existing

00:14:31.779 --> 00:14:32.779
stakes.

00:14:32.779 --> 00:14:36.319
So that we employ things like confirmation
bias, seeking information consistent with

00:14:36.319 --> 00:14:38.329
our current points of view.

00:14:38.329 --> 00:14:39.939
We employ hindsight bias.

00:14:39.939 --> 00:14:44.699
Once we see results, we look at the results
that are best for us and say, oh of course.

00:14:44.699 --> 00:14:46.350
That's how we would have done this study.

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:48.419
That's the right kind of result to pay attention
to.

00:14:48.419 --> 00:14:51.119
That's what I would have expected to have
occurred.

00:14:51.119 --> 00:14:55.540
And so all of those reasoning strategies many
unintentionally-- without even being aware

00:14:55.540 --> 00:15:00.189
of that I'm employing them-- may be shaping
the literature coming out of my lab to look

00:15:00.189 --> 00:15:01.820
better than it actually is.

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:07.129
A third barrier is that a lot of that happens
with very little accountability.

00:15:07.129 --> 00:15:09.410
None of you know what happens in my lab.

00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:13.480
All you get to see is the reports of the end.

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:17.379
And so I don't have the conditions, the situation,
of perspective-taking.

00:15:17.379 --> 00:15:21.649
Of, as we are reasoning through, trying to
do our research, of thinking about how would

00:15:21.649 --> 00:15:25.699
an observer evaluate the decisions that we're
making here?

00:15:25.699 --> 00:15:27.260
The decision to drop that study?

00:15:27.260 --> 00:15:28.670
To amplify that analysis?

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:29.670
To suppress that analysis?

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:33.619
Would they think that that is a reasonable
thing to do?

00:15:33.619 --> 00:15:39.029
And as we know from a lot of research on accountability,
that perspective-taking, or knowing that someone

00:15:39.029 --> 00:15:43.939
could observe what I'm doing fundamentally
changes how I think about those problems.

00:15:43.939 --> 00:15:49.119
What decisions I make about what I'm going
to do and how I'm going to do it.

00:15:49.119 --> 00:15:51.290
And then lastly, we're very busy.

00:15:51.290 --> 00:15:52.919
We're all very busy.

00:15:52.919 --> 00:15:57.729
And so the ideas of being more transparent,
of making your data more accessible, of opening

00:15:57.729 --> 00:16:03.449
up our materials, our process of research--
all of those are perceived-- and in reality,

00:16:03.449 --> 00:16:06.439
many times-- as an extra work.

00:16:06.439 --> 00:16:10.019
As an appendage on the existing work flows.

00:16:10.019 --> 00:16:15.600
And if-- in a competitive landscape like science--
in a place where we are over-committed already

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:20.979
with a variety of projects that we're trying
to pursue and understand and do-- we are not

00:16:20.979 --> 00:16:27.249
likely to be easily amenable to adding on
new things that we have to do in order to

00:16:27.249 --> 00:16:28.589
make that research more accessible.

00:16:28.589 --> 00:16:31.609
Especially if we're not incentivized to do
it.

00:16:31.609 --> 00:16:38.149
If there is no reward for me making my data,
my materials, my protocols more open, then

00:16:38.149 --> 00:16:41.029
why would I spend that extra time doing it?

00:16:41.029 --> 00:16:45.680
Even with the values of desiring that as a
good outcome.

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:46.939
So those are formidable barriers.

00:16:46.939 --> 00:16:52.369
And there's a lot that needs to change in
the culture in order to try to address improvements

00:16:52.369 --> 00:16:56.500
of openness and reproducibility as ordinary
practice.

00:16:56.500 --> 00:17:02.040
So there are a few areas that the Center for
Open Science focuses on to try to maximize

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:07.000
the likelihood that we can move to a more
open and reproducible research culture.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:10.429
First strategy is that we are primarily a
technology company.

00:17:10.429 --> 00:17:15.699
We have a staff of 55 and about 2/3 of that--
full-time staff 55.

00:17:15.699 --> 00:17:17.909
There's interns as well.

00:17:17.909 --> 00:17:22.069
But about 2/3 of that staff is building tools
like the open science framework to enable

00:17:22.069 --> 00:17:25.339
researchers to be more open with their research.

00:17:25.339 --> 00:17:30.870
We also provide training, as many others do,
for how to be more open and reproducible in

00:17:30.870 --> 00:17:32.730
research practices.

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:37.750
And then the third is providing some means
of shifting, nudging those incentives that

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:44.419
drive our behavior-- through funders, through
journals, through institutions-- so that researchers

00:17:44.419 --> 00:17:49.230
have a reason to embrace moving toward more
open practices.

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:53.960
So I'd like to do for the rest of the time
that we have is to summarize some of our activities

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:54.960
of these.

00:17:54.960 --> 00:18:00.340
And then we'll be delighted to have some questions
and discussion about what other barriers or

00:18:00.340 --> 00:18:05.640
what opportunities there are to try to align
scientific values and practices more closely.

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:12.640
Let me start with the big picture view of
how do we shift incentives, given the cultural

00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:15.620
challenges that we face in science.

00:18:15.620 --> 00:18:17.690
And we can characterize this in very simple
terms.

00:18:17.690 --> 00:18:23.049
Sort of a psychology 101 perspective on scientific
behavior.

00:18:23.049 --> 00:18:27.840
And that is to think about what are the means
with which researchers get the rewards that

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:28.840
they get.

00:18:28.840 --> 00:18:31.830
And those being the primary drivers of behavior.

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:37.840
So right now, my primary means of getting
the key reward-- publication-- are focused

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:39.559
on the outcomes of the
research.

00:18:39.559 --> 00:18:43.670
The things that we do in the lab, the more
likely we are to have novel, positive, clean

00:18:43.670 --> 00:18:49.280
results, the more likely I am to get that
reward that I need-- publication and publication

00:18:49.280 --> 00:18:50.970
in prestigious outlets.

00:18:50.970 --> 00:18:57.940
So if this is the singular model of incentive,
of reward, then everything hinges on outcomes.

00:18:57.940 --> 00:19:04.580
On producing amazing, sexy, exciting results,
and everything else is essentially irrelevant

00:19:04.580 --> 00:19:07.590
to how it is I advance in my career.

00:19:07.590 --> 00:19:11.759
So how do we change that model so that it
isn't just a singular reward system?

00:19:11.759 --> 00:19:15.750
Well one obvious thing to do is add additional
means.

00:19:15.750 --> 00:19:19.950
So instead of me just being rewarded on the
outcomes that I produce, can we also nudge

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:25.880
those incentives so that sometimes, in part,
I'm awarded on the process by which I pursue

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:26.880
those outcomes.

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:27.880
Right?

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:33.549
The quality of my methodology, the accessibility,
the transparency of that methodology.

00:19:33.549 --> 00:19:37.950
Evidence for reproducibility of the process
by which I generate my findings.

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:43.720
If those can also be a mechanism of reward,
then it isn't just the outcomes.

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:48.169
Although we will never get rid of outcomes
because people who discover more amazing things

00:19:48.169 --> 00:19:50.769
will be rewarded more than people who don't.

00:19:50.769 --> 00:19:55.659
But we can add additional means of getting
rewards so that the process also is part of

00:19:55.659 --> 00:19:57.580
career advancement.

00:19:57.580 --> 00:20:00.370
Likewise, we can diversify the rewards.

00:20:00.370 --> 00:20:06.419
So it isn't just getting publication and having
the journal article be the sole mechanism

00:20:06.419 --> 00:20:07.419
of advancement.

00:20:07.419 --> 00:20:12.350
But also, the generation of important research
content, right?

00:20:12.350 --> 00:20:19.039
So a person who generates an amazingly important
data set should be rewarded for that as a

00:20:19.039 --> 00:20:20.039
scholarly unit of its own.

00:20:20.039 --> 00:20:24.019
The data being a mechanism of scholarly advancement.

00:20:24.019 --> 00:20:29.000
The materials, the analytic scripts and code
that are generated are themselves scholarly

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:34.629
contributions and could be credited as such,
rather than having it solely be through the

00:20:34.629 --> 00:20:41.310
getting your name on a paper, and particularly--
getting your name first or last on that paper.

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:47.190
So most of our strategies are thinking about
ways to diversify means and rewards across

00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:52.710
the existing incentive structures that drive
researchers' behaviors today.

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:58.059
So let me start with an example that's a trivially
easy or simple one.

00:20:58.059 --> 00:21:01.230
And that is to signal desirable behaviors.

00:21:01.230 --> 00:21:06.570
So this is an example of badges to reward
open practices.

00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:10.700
So we developed these badges and there's a
committee of researchers from a variety of

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:15.590
different fields that help maintain the specifications
for what it means to have open data, or open

00:21:15.590 --> 00:21:18.130
materials, or have preregistered your research
design.

00:21:18.130 --> 00:21:21.909
Define in advance what you're doing and how
you're going to do it.

00:21:21.909 --> 00:21:24.200
A journal can adopt these badges.

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:29.100
And then when my paper gets accepted, can
invite me to say, would you like any of these

00:21:29.100 --> 00:21:32.299
badges to be attached to your paper?

00:21:32.299 --> 00:21:34.100
So it appears there.

00:21:34.100 --> 00:21:36.840
And if you can meet the specifications, then
you get the badge.

00:21:36.840 --> 00:21:41.360
And then there would be a link, for example,
with open data to-- how someone can access

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:43.900
your data in an independent repository.

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:45.320
No badges are silly.

00:21:45.320 --> 00:21:47.789
They are a trivial thing.

00:21:47.789 --> 00:21:53.139
But in this case, they are signals of something
that's valued in the research community.

00:21:53.139 --> 00:21:57.679
Openness and valued, but no one knows when
someone else is being open in their

00:21:57.679 --> 00:21:58.879
practices, right?

00:21:58.879 --> 00:22:04.150
There aren't any signals for doing those extra
things to be more open or transparent in one's

00:22:04.150 --> 00:22:05.150
work.

00:22:05.150 --> 00:22:10.510
And so providing a signal of evidence that
someone is doing a desirable behavior-- especially

00:22:10.510 --> 00:22:14.940
when it's not easy to see otherwise-- could
be a powerful shift.

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:19.870
Very simple, but powerful shift in the incentives
that drive individual researcher's behaviors.

00:22:19.870 --> 00:22:25.110
They may be more willing to do those extra
steps just to be able to signal that they

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:29.870
care about these issues, that they are pursuing
open practices.

00:22:29.870 --> 00:22:35.279
So we worked with the journal Psychological
Science to-- as the first journal that adopted

00:22:35.279 --> 00:22:36.279
badges.

00:22:36.279 --> 00:22:39.070
And they adopted on January 1st, 2014.

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:44.639
And what I'm showing you here is an investigation
of the effect of badges-- this new policy

00:22:44.639 --> 00:22:52.149
of offering badges at Psychological Science,
compared to four comparison journals in psychology.

00:22:52.149 --> 00:22:58.490
And the y-axis is showing the percent of articles
for every half year, from 2012 to the end

00:22:58.490 --> 00:23:03.299
of 2013, for the percent of articles that
said the data were available in an independent

00:23:03.299 --> 00:23:04.299
repository.

00:23:04.299 --> 00:23:09.190
And Psychological Science, in black, shows
about 3% of their articles, on average, each

00:23:09.190 --> 00:23:13.081
half year were-- had data available in a repository.

00:23:13.081 --> 00:23:16.120
And the other journals, likewise, had low
amounts.

00:23:16.120 --> 00:23:22.950
So then, Psychological Science adopts badges
January 1st, 2014, and then this is what happens.

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:29.289
So by the first half of 2015, 39% of the articles
in Psychological Science had open data in

00:23:29.289 --> 00:23:30.289
a repository.

00:23:30.289 --> 00:23:35.710
And the only change was the introduction of
this policy-- of the opportunity to apply

00:23:35.710 --> 00:23:38.260
for a badge on the article.

00:23:38.260 --> 00:23:43.710
It has no implication, other than signaling
to the others in your research community,

00:23:43.710 --> 00:23:46.350
that you did this particular practice.

00:23:46.350 --> 00:23:55.460
So it is a trivial change, but it had an inordinately
strong impact on behavior-- 13-fold increase

00:23:55.460 --> 00:24:00.170
the rates of data sharing-- at least in this
example.

00:24:00.170 --> 00:24:04.309
So that-- it isn't magical abilities in any
particular way.

00:24:04.309 --> 00:24:07.360
It's just leveraging the fact that the values
already exist.

00:24:07.360 --> 00:24:13.519
As I showed you on that first slide of Anderson
and her colleagues, people already endorse

00:24:13.519 --> 00:24:14.830
the norm of openness.

00:24:14.830 --> 00:24:17.399
They're just not rewarded for behaving that
way.

00:24:17.399 --> 00:24:23.129
So by providing a simple signal, that can
dramatically increase people's likelihood

00:24:23.129 --> 00:24:26.740
of adhering to the values that they already
have.

00:24:26.740 --> 00:24:30.320
And in doing so in effective ways.

00:24:30.320 --> 00:24:31.750
OK so that's one example.

00:24:31.750 --> 00:24:38.559
To introduce the next example, I want to just
give a research example of this challenge

00:24:38.559 --> 00:24:42.860
of selective reporting and how it can make
the published literature look more credible

00:24:42.860 --> 00:24:43.860
than it is.

00:24:43.860 --> 00:24:49.049
And there's lots of good research examples
in clinical trials and other fields with this.

00:24:49.049 --> 00:24:52.190
This example comes from the social sciences.

00:24:52.190 --> 00:24:58.860
And what I'm showing you here is a comparison
of studies that were preregistered and what

00:24:58.860 --> 00:25:04.049
the methodology was going to be done, versus
what was actually reported in the paper that

00:25:04.049 --> 00:25:05.860
came out in the end.

00:25:05.860 --> 00:25:12.040
So Annie Franco and her colleagues took advantage
of a NSF-funded project called TESS- this

00:25:12.040 --> 00:25:15.299
is Time Series Experiments in Social Sciences.

00:25:15.299 --> 00:25:23.210
And what TESS is it's a coordinated effort
to give researchers in the disciplines access

00:25:23.210 --> 00:25:26.309
to a representative sample of adults to do
experiments.

00:25:26.309 --> 00:25:28.120
Those are very precious samples.

00:25:28.120 --> 00:25:30.480
They're hard to get and recruit and maintain.

00:25:30.480 --> 00:25:32.559
And so this is a valued resource.

00:25:32.559 --> 00:25:36.960
So what researchers in the field can do is
they can design their protocol and then they

00:25:36.960 --> 00:25:39.710
submit it to TESS for peer review.

00:25:39.710 --> 00:25:46.679
And if TESS accepts it, then TESS runs that
protocol on this sample of-- representative

00:25:46.679 --> 00:25:50.740
sample of adults, and then sends you the data.

00:25:50.740 --> 00:25:55.879
The condition for the researchers getting
access to this as a free resource is that

00:25:55.879 --> 00:25:59.300
you have to make your protocol public and
the data public so that others can review

00:25:59.300 --> 00:26:00.300
it.

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:04.749
And so what it creates is this opportunity
to see what was actually in the research.

00:26:04.749 --> 00:26:10.330
Because we almost never-- in basic and preclinical
sciences-- almost never know what the precise

00:26:10.330 --> 00:26:12.490
methodology was, just what was in the report.

00:26:12.490 --> 00:26:16.610
Versus what ends up in the report-- what do
they say they did in their project?

00:26:16.610 --> 00:26:23.280
And so what I'm showing you on the left here--
on the x-axis is the number of experimental

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:28.519
conditions or treatment arms that were actually
in the study, from zero to five in the questionnaire--

00:26:28.519 --> 00:26:30.590
in the data as it was collected.

00:26:30.590 --> 00:26:35.990
And the y-axis is the number of experimental
conditions or treatment arms that were reported

00:26:35.990 --> 00:26:36.990
in the paper.

00:26:36.990 --> 00:26:40.190
Where they said that these conditions did
occur.

00:26:40.190 --> 00:26:44.930
And what you can see is that if every paper
reported all of the conditions that were in

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:49.480
the experiment, then all of the dots would
be on the diagonal line.

00:26:49.480 --> 00:26:53.470
The 40% of the studies in this case left out
one or more of the experimental conditions--

00:26:53.470 --> 00:26:58.370
just didn't mention that they had occurred
in the paper, even though they had occurred

00:26:58.370 --> 00:27:00.200
in the protocol.

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:05.120
On the panel on the right is the same thing,
but now with the outcome variables.

00:27:05.120 --> 00:27:09.440
So on the x-axis is showing the number of
outcome variables that were actually in the

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:10.759
experiment as it was conducted.

00:27:10.759 --> 00:27:16.540
And on the y-axis is the number of outcome
variables that were reported in the paper,

00:27:16.540 --> 00:27:19.270
at the end in the published article.

00:27:19.270 --> 00:27:25.779
70% of the articles left out one or more of
the outcome variables that was actually in

00:27:25.779 --> 00:27:27.289
the study.

00:27:27.289 --> 00:27:31.169
Now it's possible that that happens-- that
happens for a lot of different reasons.

00:27:31.169 --> 00:27:36.179
And it may not be consequential if there's
no particular bias in what's likely to be

00:27:36.179 --> 00:27:40.320
reported in the paper versus what's likely
to be left out.

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:44.419
But because this is open data, Franco and
her colleagues were able to follow up and

00:27:44.419 --> 00:27:49.419
look at what was included and what was excluded
from the paper.

00:27:49.419 --> 00:27:54.730
And what they found was that for the 122 reported
tests that were included in the paper, the

00:27:54.730 --> 00:27:58.369
median p value was 0.02, so they were positive
results, right?

00:27:58.369 --> 00:28:04.220
P less than 0.05 is the standard metric for
deciding whether you obtain a positive result.

00:28:04.220 --> 00:28:10.580
The median effect size was 0.29, so almost
a third of a standard deviation, approximately.

00:28:10.580 --> 00:28:13.340
And 63% of the results that were reported
were positive results.

00:28:13.340 --> 00:28:17.260
So then they went-- because they have the
access to the raw data-- to the result the

00:28:17.260 --> 00:28:18.880
outcome variables that were reported.

00:28:18.880 --> 00:28:22.149
And what they observed is that there were
147 of those.

00:28:22.149 --> 00:28:24.289
The median p value was 0.35.

00:28:24.289 --> 00:28:26.759
The median effect size was 0.13, less than
half.

00:28:26.759 --> 00:28:30.679
And only 23% of those were significant effects.

00:28:30.679 --> 00:28:37.020
So this suggests that there is a substantial
bias in what outcomes are likely to get reported

00:28:37.020 --> 00:28:38.020
in the paper.

00:28:38.020 --> 00:28:40.410
Positive
outcomes, compared to the outcomes that were

00:28:40.410 --> 00:28:45.299
actually observed, suggesting that the reported
research-- that the papers at the end were

00:28:45.299 --> 00:28:50.490
more beautiful, more clean, better stories
than the data actually suggested.

00:28:50.490 --> 00:28:56.480
So that's a significant challenge and it's
only identifiable because we can tell the

00:28:56.480 --> 00:29:01.929
difference between what was planned at the
outset and what was done and reported at the

00:29:01.929 --> 00:29:03.330
end.

00:29:03.330 --> 00:29:07.159
And this is related to a very important distinction
in the philosophy of science, which is two

00:29:07.159 --> 00:29:09.720
different modes of research, right?

00:29:09.720 --> 00:29:13.340
In one mode, we are in the context of justification.

00:29:13.340 --> 00:29:19.360
We have a hypothesis that we want to acquire
data to test.

00:29:19.360 --> 00:29:26.140
And so this is-- in hypothesis-testing mode,
we are in confirmatory approach to analysis,

00:29:26.140 --> 00:29:27.799
right?

00:29:27.799 --> 00:29:32.460
We are laying out a hypothesis, a methodology,
a way we're going to analyze the data that

00:29:32.460 --> 00:29:34.149
we obtain from that methodology.

00:29:34.149 --> 00:29:38.909
And then we go out and get data and then confront
our hypothesis with the data.

00:29:38.909 --> 00:29:43.580
So the decisions, in this case, about the
hypothesis and the analysis plan, are independent

00:29:43.580 --> 00:29:44.580
of the data itself.

00:29:44.580 --> 00:29:48.159
You don't get to know what the data are because
you have to make your commitments to what

00:29:48.159 --> 00:29:54.460
your hypothesis is and how you would analyze
it in advance in order to confront that hypothesis.

00:29:54.460 --> 00:29:58.009
The other mode of analysis is when we're in
the context of discovery.

00:29:58.009 --> 00:30:01.240
Where we're using data in order to generate
hypotheses.

00:30:01.240 --> 00:30:06.499
We're getting in the weeds and looking at
data where we don't have a clear sense of

00:30:06.499 --> 00:30:08.440
a hypothesis or a overall theory.

00:30:08.440 --> 00:30:12.669
And we're exploring that in order to see where
things might occur.

00:30:12.669 --> 00:30:19.789
So that once we can generate those hypotheses--
once we have ideas-- we can then later confront

00:30:19.789 --> 00:30:24.419
them, do a different study in order to test
those hypotheses.

00:30:24.419 --> 00:30:27.720
Those decisions, in the context of exploration,
are data-contingent.

00:30:27.720 --> 00:30:33.670
We're using data to inform how we are analyzing
the data.

00:30:33.670 --> 00:30:38.129
The importance of this distinction is-- it's
important for a number of reasons.

00:30:38.129 --> 00:30:43.909
But there is a practice that is central to
making clear when we're in one mode of analysis

00:30:43.909 --> 00:30:44.909
or the other.

00:30:44.909 --> 00:30:48.679
And both of these modes are critical for scientific
advancement.

00:30:48.679 --> 00:30:54.909
And the key thing that preregistration does
is clarify when we are in the context of discovery

00:30:54.909 --> 00:30:57.730
versus the context of justification.

00:30:57.730 --> 00:31:00.970
Because mixing these two up has important
implications.

00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:03.190
So why is this distinction important?

00:31:03.190 --> 00:31:10.909
Well, the typical tools of statistical inference--
using p-values, for example, in null hypothesis

00:31:10.909 --> 00:31:15.350
significance testing, are interpretable only
in the context of confirmation.

00:31:15.350 --> 00:31:20.760
Where you know precisely the number and kind
of inference tests you are going to do, and

00:31:20.760 --> 00:31:25.830
then can apply corrective processes for the
number of tests versus the likelihood of them

00:31:25.830 --> 00:31:27.960
being false positives.

00:31:27.960 --> 00:31:32.119
But when we're in the context of discovery--
when we're making choices contingent on the

00:31:32.119 --> 00:31:34.440
data-- p-values lose their diagnosticity.

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:38.539
They don't have the informative [INAUDIBLE]
that they have, because there are many possible

00:31:38.539 --> 00:31:42.980
analyses we could have done that we either
did do and ignored or didn't do because we

00:31:42.980 --> 00:31:46.470
didn't think to do them, given what we saw
in the data itself.

00:31:46.470 --> 00:31:49.950
Andrew Gelman, for example, calls this the
garden of forking paths.

00:31:49.950 --> 00:31:54.759
When you're confronted with many possibilities
as you observe the data, many of those paths

00:31:54.759 --> 00:31:58.869
wither away very easily and quickly and you
never pursue them.

00:31:58.869 --> 00:32:02.960
If you hadn't known the data, you might have
pursued those tests.

00:32:02.960 --> 00:32:08.240
And so the-- that causes-- the fact that the
p-values are not interpretable in that context--

00:32:08.240 --> 00:32:14.369
they're non-diagnostic increases the likelihood
of false discoveries when you're in exploratory

00:32:14.369 --> 00:32:17.980
mode when you are using p-values to draw differences.

00:32:17.980 --> 00:32:24.869
So the key challenge for not distinguishing,
via preregistration, when you're in a context

00:32:24.869 --> 00:32:30.970
of justification versus discovery is that
presenting exploratory results as confirmatory

00:32:30.970 --> 00:32:32.429
can increase the publishability, right?

00:32:32.429 --> 00:32:34.110
I see more positive results.

00:32:34.110 --> 00:32:35.340
I get more interested in those.

00:32:35.340 --> 00:32:38.480
I start to draw interpretations and theories
that explain those.

00:32:38.480 --> 00:32:42.880
But at the cost of the credibility-- of the
likelihood that they are false positives and

00:32:42.880 --> 00:32:45.960
not aligned with reality.

00:32:45.960 --> 00:32:51.440
So in the ideal model of how research operates,
because we're doing all of these simultaneously,

00:32:51.440 --> 00:32:57.360
is one where we would generate a study-- where
we do studies for some reason.

00:32:57.360 --> 00:32:59.669
And we write down, why is it that we are doing
this study?

00:32:59.669 --> 00:33:01.370
What is the purpose of this study?

00:33:01.370 --> 00:33:03.470
What are we trying to find or infer?

00:33:03.470 --> 00:33:08.720
And many times we get into a new domain with
very little idea of where it might go.

00:33:08.720 --> 00:33:12.369
We have some ideas, so it may be a very simple--
we're going to try this one thing to start.

00:33:12.369 --> 00:33:15.470
But really this is an exploratory investigation.

00:33:15.470 --> 00:33:17.029
So we write down what that is.

00:33:17.029 --> 00:33:22.100
We commit to that in advance is our very constrained
view of what we're testing as a hypothesis.

00:33:22.100 --> 00:33:24.139
And this is now routine in my lab.

00:33:24.139 --> 00:33:26.850
We pre-register everything.

00:33:26.850 --> 00:33:31.529
Once we've analyzed the data in that very
simple way that we started, then we take that

00:33:31.529 --> 00:33:33.970
data set and we explore the heck out of it.

00:33:33.970 --> 00:33:39.090
We move into exploratory mode of analysis
where we are trying to now unpack this phenomenon.

00:33:39.090 --> 00:33:43.580
Where we had some interesting questions, but
we had very little idea of what direction

00:33:43.580 --> 00:33:44.989
to go.

00:33:44.989 --> 00:33:50.020
And that exploratory mode of analysis gives
us now some ideas, some hypotheses, that we

00:33:50.020 --> 00:33:53.080
can start to shape the phenomenon that we're
investigating.

00:33:53.080 --> 00:33:55.470
That then inspires the next study.

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:59.320
And in that study, we can preregister our
design.

00:33:59.320 --> 00:34:03.639
Now that we have some hypotheses pre-registered,
what we're going to do to test that and how

00:34:03.639 --> 00:34:05.129
we're going to analyze it.

00:34:05.129 --> 00:34:08.810
And then move right back into exploratory
mode once we discover how wrong we were that

00:34:08.810 --> 00:34:09.810
time, too.

00:34:09.810 --> 00:34:12.950
And then back and forth and back and forth.

00:34:12.950 --> 00:34:20.250
So this concept of pre-registration is well
known now with clinical sciences because it's

00:34:20.250 --> 00:34:23.280
required for certain types of clinical trials.

00:34:23.280 --> 00:34:29.169
But it is relatively unknown and certainly
not practiced in most basic and pre-clinical

00:34:29.169 --> 00:34:31.480
areas of research.

00:34:31.480 --> 00:34:38.970
And even though the value may be seen as clear,
there is lack of familiarity because it often

00:34:38.970 --> 00:34:44.270
was disruptive to the workflows that we have
for how it is we do our research.

00:34:44.270 --> 00:34:49.080
Whether it's experimental data questions,
whether it's working with existing data sets,

00:34:49.080 --> 00:34:55.290
or otherwise, there are lots of different
ways in which we can create better distinctions

00:34:55.290 --> 00:34:59.210
between our confirmatory and exploratory modes
of analysis.

00:34:59.210 --> 00:35:04.250
So one initiative that we have to try to promote
awareness and education about the value of

00:35:04.250 --> 00:35:07.210
preregistration is called the preregistration
challenge.

00:35:07.210 --> 00:35:10.319
And you can visit it at the link here.

00:35:10.319 --> 00:35:16.640
The idea of the pre-registration challenge
is that we are giving 1,000 $1,000 awards

00:35:16.640 --> 00:35:22.069
to the first 1,000 researchers that publish
the results of a preregistered study, in an

00:35:22.069 --> 00:35:23.079
eligible journal.

00:35:23.079 --> 00:35:26.809
Really, the goal for this is an awareness
and education campaign.

00:35:26.809 --> 00:35:30.270
So when you go to the website, you get lots
of information about what preregistration

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:36.750
is, how it works, some background reading
materials, process information.

00:35:36.750 --> 00:35:42.579
And then there is a guided workflow that helps
researchers sort of take the steps for how

00:35:42.579 --> 00:35:47.181
they can pre-register their study and actually
walk them through preregistration of a research

00:35:47.181 --> 00:35:48.640
design.

00:35:48.640 --> 00:35:54.530
And then a very rapid review process to confirm
that all of the things that were-- were preregistered.

00:35:54.530 --> 00:35:57.400
And then the person goes ahead and does their
research.

00:35:57.400 --> 00:36:04.750
So our suspicion, our expectation, is that
once researchers try preregistration for their

00:36:04.750 --> 00:36:09.480
area of work-- because it's a high barrier
to initiate, because it is a disruption to

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:10.480
the ordinary workflow.

00:36:10.480 --> 00:36:14.869
But once they try it, it is relatively likely
become a habit.

00:36:14.869 --> 00:36:19.710
Because very quickly, one can experience the
benefits of preregistration.

00:36:19.710 --> 00:36:24.680
I've mentioned before that my lab does preregistration
for all the research we do now.

00:36:24.680 --> 00:36:27.119
And the benefits are very high.

00:36:27.119 --> 00:36:32.780
Because we do a lot more upfront planning
and identification of problems before we actually

00:36:32.780 --> 00:36:34.520
initiate data collection.

00:36:34.520 --> 00:36:39.730
So we end up being more efficient, not less,
because of that planning process.

00:36:39.730 --> 00:36:43.750
We've had a number of times where we thought
we had a design that was going to be effective

00:36:43.750 --> 00:36:48.160
for testing the hypothesis that we were looking
to investigate in the first place and then

00:36:48.160 --> 00:36:51.550
discovered we had some flaw in the design
that wouldn't have allowed us to test it the

00:36:51.550 --> 00:36:53.109
way that we wanted.

00:36:53.109 --> 00:36:56.480
So having done that in advance and actually
writing it down, writing down our analysis

00:36:56.480 --> 00:37:01.640
plan, confronts us with that without the waste
of the data collection.

00:37:01.640 --> 00:37:06.380
And likewise, for those areas of research
where there's a lot of variations, the paradigmatic

00:37:06.380 --> 00:37:10.369
approach in the methodology-- pre-registration
is very efficient.

00:37:10.369 --> 00:37:16.079
Because we preregister-- we develop the protocol
for one study and then we just adapt it incrementally

00:37:16.079 --> 00:37:19.410
with the experiments as they go through the
sequence of research that we do.

00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:23.920
So that's something that's worth trying out
if you have any interest at all.

00:37:23.920 --> 00:37:29.839
There is another related initiative that really
tries to get into the incentives that researchers

00:37:29.839 --> 00:37:33.359
have driving them now in that publication
process.

00:37:33.359 --> 00:37:36.040
And that's-- this one is called registered
reports.

00:37:36.040 --> 00:37:39.030
And you can go to the link there to find out
more information.

00:37:39.030 --> 00:37:43.270
So the conts-- the contents of registered
reports-- this is the cartoon version of how

00:37:43.270 --> 00:37:44.270
we do research, right?

00:37:44.270 --> 00:37:47.160
Design a study, collect and analyze the data,
write up a report, and publish it.

00:37:47.160 --> 00:37:49.530
But of course, it's not that simple.

00:37:49.530 --> 00:37:54.740
There is that barrier of peer review, that's
in my way from once I finish that report to

00:37:54.740 --> 00:37:56.960
actually publishing it.

00:37:56.960 --> 00:38:03.190
Now in this standard model, the-- all of the
incentives for me to get publication that

00:38:03.190 --> 00:38:08.180
I need are making that report
as likely as possible to make it through peer

00:38:08.180 --> 00:38:09.180
review.

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:13.059
So I need it to be a novel story, it needs
to be clean and nice, any positive results.

00:38:13.059 --> 00:38:18.020
Everything that peer reviewers will not hate,
I have to do and try to get that report looking

00:38:18.020 --> 00:38:19.230
as good as possible.

00:38:19.230 --> 00:38:25.820
Likewise, for peer reviewers, their incentives
are tied into their commitments to the findings

00:38:25.820 --> 00:38:27.070
that I'm reporting.

00:38:27.070 --> 00:38:32.770
If I'm in a contentious area of research,
and I get peer reviewers that like what I

00:38:32.770 --> 00:38:36.040
found, they may also like the methodology.

00:38:36.040 --> 00:38:38.010
That reinforces their worldview.

00:38:38.010 --> 00:38:42.220
Whereas, if they don't like what I found--
if it challenges a particular finding that

00:38:42.220 --> 00:38:46.290
they've had, or a particular perspective,
then they may be much more likely to identify

00:38:46.290 --> 00:38:50.810
all kinds of problems in the methodology,
reinforcing their negative view of the results

00:38:50.810 --> 00:38:52.569
itself.

00:38:52.569 --> 00:38:58.280
So what register reports does is shift these
incentives by making one simple change to

00:38:58.280 --> 00:38:59.829
the review process.

00:38:59.829 --> 00:39:05.700
And that is moving peer review at the journal
to right after the design phase.

00:39:05.700 --> 00:39:10.930
So what I do as an author, when I submit a
registered report to a journal, is I submit

00:39:10.930 --> 00:39:16.260
the research question, the method-- the background
for why that's an important question, and

00:39:16.260 --> 00:39:19.500
the methodology that I'm going to use to test
that question.

00:39:19.500 --> 00:39:23.171
And I might have some preliminary experiments,
for example, showing the viability of the

00:39:23.171 --> 00:39:25.530
approach or something.

00:39:25.530 --> 00:39:30.790
But the results are not known because those
experiments have not been done.

00:39:30.790 --> 00:39:35.710
And the peer reviewers evaluate the quality
of the question, the importance of the question,

00:39:35.710 --> 00:39:37.940
and the quality of a methodology to [? tackle
?] question.

00:39:37.940 --> 00:39:42.980
Do we need to know the answer to this, and
is this methodology effective, sufficient,

00:39:42.980 --> 00:39:47.980
formed to be a good way to evaluate the outcomes
of this research?

00:39:47.980 --> 00:39:53.020
And then it--that passes peer review, the
journal gives an in principle acceptance to

00:39:53.020 --> 00:39:54.020
the paper.

00:39:54.020 --> 00:39:58.200
They say, we not publish this, regardless
of whether it's a positive or negative result.

00:39:58.200 --> 00:40:06.630
As long as you did the methodology [INAUDIBLE]
with the methodology of the paper.

00:40:06.630 --> 00:40:12.650
You didn't just, you know, [INAUDIBLE] and
actually get the data, right?

00:40:12.650 --> 00:40:16.359
[INAUDIBLE]
So the key here is that it shifts incentives

00:40:16.359 --> 00:40:18.630
for everybody in the process.

00:40:18.630 --> 00:40:19.730
Just enough.

00:40:19.730 --> 00:40:22.750
So it's still about getting a publication.

00:40:22.750 --> 00:40:25.079
That's how the world works that we're in now.

00:40:25.079 --> 00:40:30.770
But the incentives for me as an author are
focused me asking the most important questions

00:40:30.770 --> 00:40:35.450
that I can and developing the most robust
methodology that I can to test them.

00:40:35.450 --> 00:40:41.799
The incentives for peer reviewers are now,
how can I evaluate this methodology?

00:40:41.799 --> 00:40:42.839
But also my feedback.

00:40:42.839 --> 00:40:46.200
Instead of saying what we did wrong and you
should have done-- in experiments already

00:40:46.200 --> 00:40:47.250
finished.

00:40:47.250 --> 00:40:49.790
Now my feedback is, how can I improve your
experiment?

00:40:49.790 --> 00:40:55.450
And so as an author I can feel more grateful
to reviewers for identifying problems at this

00:40:55.450 --> 00:41:00.400
stage because I have an opportunity to actually
fix them in the methodology before the experiments

00:41:00.400 --> 00:41:01.400
are done.

00:41:01.400 --> 00:41:05.119
So it makes the process much more collaborative.

00:41:05.119 --> 00:41:11.859
The-- so so far, we have 50 journals, I think,
that have adopted this as a publishing option,

00:41:11.859 --> 00:41:16.400
in addition to the traditional process of
research.

00:41:16.400 --> 00:41:22.680
And both-- it can't be a comprehensive replacement
because there's a lot of things that are real

00:41:22.680 --> 00:41:25.340
discoveries that aren't things that are anticipated
in advance.

00:41:25.340 --> 00:41:31.100
You don't have to study to do, but you get
a left turn, all of a sudden, in doing research.

00:41:31.100 --> 00:41:37.750
And a lot of those will appear in the normal
publication process as it occurs now.

00:41:37.750 --> 00:41:40.630
Or would be things that happened in the introduction
for one of these kinds of papers.

00:41:40.630 --> 00:41:45.060
And then you do a follow-up experiment to
really nail that phenomenon that came out

00:41:45.060 --> 00:41:49.700
of the blue during that line of research.

00:41:49.700 --> 00:41:50.710
OK.

00:41:50.710 --> 00:41:57.280
So those examples-- I give you badges, and
preregistration challenge, and registered

00:41:57.280 --> 00:42:02.470
reports-- are some efforts to try to nudge
a variety of the different incentives so that

00:42:02.470 --> 00:42:07.579
researchers are rewarded for being more open
in their practice and trying to pursue practices

00:42:07.579 --> 00:42:10.660
that are likely to increase the reproducibility
of the work.

00:42:10.660 --> 00:42:16.319
And trying to fit those into the existing
reinforcements and reward systems that researchers

00:42:16.319 --> 00:42:18.470
are living by.

00:42:18.470 --> 00:42:23.369
The other half, or most of what we spend our
time on is the technology development to support

00:42:23.369 --> 00:42:24.693
researchers to be more open in their research.

00:42:24.693 --> 00:42:30.050
And so I want to spend the rest of the time
that I have to give you an overview of what

00:42:30.050 --> 00:42:34.880
we're trying to do with the technology to
sort of support the shift towards more open

00:42:34.880 --> 00:42:36.260
practices.

00:42:36.260 --> 00:42:42.670
And really that, at the core of this, is not--
is a very simple idea for how we can improve

00:42:42.670 --> 00:42:43.670
research.

00:42:43.670 --> 00:42:50.109
And that is one that was instructed to us
in kindergarten for how we should behave.

00:42:50.109 --> 00:42:52.600
And that is to show our work and to share.

00:42:52.600 --> 00:42:58.960
There there's very little else that we really
need to do at a system level, I think, to

00:42:58.960 --> 00:43:00.470
improve research practices.

00:43:00.470 --> 00:43:05.420
One of the great things about science is that
it's an open marketplace of ideas.

00:43:05.420 --> 00:43:10.490
People are seeing ideas, being skeptical of
others' ideas, challenging them, extending

00:43:10.490 --> 00:43:12.720
them, re-using them, transforming them.

00:43:12.720 --> 00:43:19.930
And over time, some stuff pops out of that
brew of marketplace as surviving-- as getting

00:43:19.930 --> 00:43:25.500
through that skeptical process and having
some value beyond just the exploration of

00:43:25.500 --> 00:43:27.530
the idea itself.

00:43:27.530 --> 00:43:33.109
For that process to work efficiently, main
thing that we need to know is what are the

00:43:33.109 --> 00:43:34.670
basis of people's claims?

00:43:34.670 --> 00:43:38.060
How are they arriving at those claims so that
I can actually critique them?

00:43:38.060 --> 00:43:40.650
So that that process can self-correct?

00:43:40.650 --> 00:43:44.950
Because without access to the background information,
we don't know how to self-correct.

00:43:44.950 --> 00:43:49.430
We don't know how to critique the findings
as effectively as we can.

00:43:49.430 --> 00:43:55.339
And so if nothing else, if we can make that
process more accessible and more transparent,

00:43:55.339 --> 00:44:01.210
more open, than self-correction will work
as we imagine it all could.

00:44:01.210 --> 00:44:08.750
So that openness for-- from our perspective,
applies to three different areas of practice.

00:44:08.750 --> 00:44:11.010
Open access is very familiar, right?

00:44:11.010 --> 00:44:16.550
Papers-- making papers more accessible to
more people, rather than being behind, for

00:44:16.550 --> 00:44:18.910
example, subscription services.

00:44:18.910 --> 00:44:22.550
Open data is a very prominent and ongoing
discussion now.

00:44:22.550 --> 00:44:26.799
Is how do we make the content of our research--
the data, the materials, the protocols, the

00:44:26.799 --> 00:44:29.630
code-- more accessible to others?

00:44:29.630 --> 00:44:34.250
But a critical third piece, that isn't yet
as much of a discussion, is open workflow.

00:44:34.250 --> 00:44:39.411
The process by which I generate those data
and process by which I analyze this data and

00:44:39.411 --> 00:44:40.411
draw inference.

00:44:40.411 --> 00:44:43.380
Can we make that more accessible for others?

00:44:43.380 --> 00:44:49.059
Because that process, like preregistration,
is just as important as the data itself to

00:44:49.059 --> 00:44:53.240
understand the basis of claims that come out
of it.

00:44:53.240 --> 00:45:00.349
So all three of those are part of the various
approaches to building technology infrastructure

00:45:00.349 --> 00:45:03.760
tools to help researchers do their research.

00:45:03.760 --> 00:45:09.120
And all of this is organized through the Open
Science Framework, and that's at osf.io.

00:45:09.120 --> 00:45:13.670
All of the tools we build are free and open
source-- we build exclusively public goods.

00:45:13.670 --> 00:45:19.571
And the Open Science Framework is a scholarly
commons that has as its first mission to help

00:45:19.571 --> 00:45:23.440
researchers manage their own research for
their own use-- privately.

00:45:23.440 --> 00:45:26.690
So when you go to the OSF, you can sign up
for an account.

00:45:26.690 --> 00:45:32.460
When you have an account, you can easily create
projects, you add materials and data-- things

00:45:32.460 --> 00:45:34.550
that you're working on those projects to those.

00:45:34.550 --> 00:45:39.400
Those are all then stored in cloud-based archival
services.

00:45:39.400 --> 00:45:43.760
You can add collaborators to those projects,
so that they have access to those work spaces

00:45:43.760 --> 00:45:45.109
as well.

00:45:45.109 --> 00:45:47.110
And all of that can be done privately.

00:45:47.110 --> 00:45:51.940
So with the OSF, you never have to make anything
public you don't want to.

00:45:51.940 --> 00:45:56.450
And the problem that we're trying to solve
with this infrastructure, by allowing this

00:45:56.450 --> 00:46:01.490
privacy, is researchers maintaining their
old data and materials for their own use,

00:46:01.490 --> 00:46:02.490
right?

00:46:02.490 --> 00:46:07.839
The person that loses the most information
from my research is me.

00:46:07.839 --> 00:46:10.020
Because we have lots of different projects
in the lab.

00:46:10.020 --> 00:46:14.020
We have lots of people coming in and out of
the lab-- postdocs and graduate students and

00:46:14.020 --> 00:46:15.140
research assistants.

00:46:15.140 --> 00:46:19.970
And when one person leaves or one computer
dies, a lot of information leaves with them.

00:46:19.970 --> 00:46:22.920
So what the OSF solves is that archival problem.

00:46:22.920 --> 00:46:29.720
Is a cloud-based solution, where every project
is stored for the lab in that space.

00:46:29.720 --> 00:46:31.540
And it also is a collective knowledge base.

00:46:31.540 --> 00:46:37.410
So that post-doc leaves, I don't lose because
of that person's ad hoc organization system.

00:46:37.410 --> 00:46:42.320
I don't lose the information, the data, the
source materials, the notebook, the electronic

00:46:42.320 --> 00:46:43.690
note book, et cetera.

00:46:43.690 --> 00:46:47.440
I don't need that information because that
person is gone, too.

00:46:47.440 --> 00:46:52.930
So the key, though, is we solve that problem--
trying to make this online resource a repository

00:46:52.930 --> 00:46:55.640
for one's lab's own use.

00:46:55.640 --> 00:47:00.720
While then simultaneously making it trivial--
make parts or all of that more accessible

00:47:00.720 --> 00:47:01.720
others.

00:47:01.720 --> 00:47:02.720
Make it open.

00:47:02.720 --> 00:47:06.410
So on every project, and every component of
every project, there's a little button that

00:47:06.410 --> 00:47:07.410
says make public.

00:47:07.410 --> 00:47:10.579
And If I click that button, it says are you
sure you want to make that public?

00:47:10.579 --> 00:47:14.230
And if I say yes, then that part of the project
is public.

00:47:14.230 --> 00:47:17.630
And so by that, we have integrated private
and public workflows.

00:47:17.630 --> 00:47:20.190
So that my lab can be doing its research.

00:47:20.190 --> 00:47:23.108
And we may have a longitudinal project that
we're going to do for many years.

00:47:23.108 --> 00:47:29.150
And we're not ready to make the data set available,
but we are ready to sort of make some of the

00:47:29.150 --> 00:47:32.789
materials-- other people could use these materials
or they might have ideas or collaborations

00:47:32.789 --> 00:47:35.590
if they see some of
the methodology that we're pursuing.

00:47:35.590 --> 00:47:39.450
So we make that part of the project public
while we continue to work with the other parts

00:47:39.450 --> 00:47:43.579
privately in the OSF with just the collaborators.

00:47:43.579 --> 00:47:49.049
So the idea is to reduce the barriers to openness--
to be trivial barriers, right?

00:47:49.049 --> 00:47:53.310
Not add on to people's workflow and make it
append to their work-- busy work days.

00:47:53.310 --> 00:47:57.530
But rather, provide a tool that has value
for maintaining your own research for your

00:47:57.530 --> 00:48:02.480
own use and make it easy to integrate that
private workflow management with making things

00:48:02.480 --> 00:48:04.950
publicly accessible.

00:48:04.950 --> 00:48:12.309
At the core of the OSF is an API to connect
services around the entire research lifecycle.

00:48:12.309 --> 00:48:17.119
And so, once a particular service is connected
to the OSF, and that service is connected

00:48:17.119 --> 00:48:21.140
to all of the other services that are already
connected to the OSF.

00:48:21.140 --> 00:48:25.720
So these are services that are connected as
of today-- Zotero and Mendeley, citation management

00:48:25.720 --> 00:48:26.720
services.

00:48:26.720 --> 00:48:32.099
Data storage, like Google Drive, box.com,
Dropbox, Amazon Web-- Amazon S3.

00:48:32.099 --> 00:48:38.359
GitHub for code, JASP stats for data analysis,
and then other archival environments like

00:48:38.359 --> 00:48:39.569
ThinkShare or Dataverse.

00:48:39.569 --> 00:48:43.819
And then we also have a number of other ones
that are in development now through grants

00:48:43.819 --> 00:48:48.599
that we provided to variety of different groups
to connect their service.

00:48:48.599 --> 00:48:54.400
The idea for this is-- one, that we can help
researchers get around their research lifecycle

00:48:54.400 --> 00:49:00.300
more efficiently, with less loss of information,
by connecting all the services they use together.

00:49:00.300 --> 00:49:06.070
And by connecting those services, make researchers
more efficient getting around the lifecycle,

00:49:06.070 --> 00:49:10.280
but also decrease the likelihood of error--
of information going in and out of the different

00:49:10.280 --> 00:49:11.380
services that you use.

00:49:11.380 --> 00:49:16.020
The analytics platform, where it's stored,
with how they visualize their data, with writing

00:49:16.020 --> 00:49:17.850
up for publication, et cetera.

00:49:17.850 --> 00:49:22.950
So if we can minimize error and increase efficiency
getting around the lifecycle, then researchers

00:49:22.950 --> 00:49:23.950
win.

00:49:23.950 --> 00:49:26.240
They get more efficient in doing their work.

00:49:26.240 --> 00:49:30.670
And by connecting all these services together,
larger collaborative teams can use the same

00:49:30.670 --> 00:49:34.589
set of services, even if only a subset of
them actually make use of them, right?

00:49:34.589 --> 00:49:37.960
So I might use GitHub because I'm familiar
with command line and code.

00:49:37.960 --> 00:49:40.740
But one of my collaborators might not use
GitHub.

00:49:40.740 --> 00:49:46.789
But by representing in the OSF and the simplified
interface, I can still give those collaborators

00:49:46.789 --> 00:49:52.970
access to the code and scripts that we're
using without them having to be a regular

00:49:52.970 --> 00:49:55.430
user of the service.

00:49:55.430 --> 00:50:00.029
And then a byproduct of all of that is that
the entire process, the entire research lifecycle,

00:50:00.029 --> 00:50:02.119
is more reproducible.

00:50:02.119 --> 00:50:06.780
Because as we've connected these together,
we can log what happens-- actually follow

00:50:06.780 --> 00:50:12.670
the process of doing the research in order
to make the entire thing more reproducible

00:50:12.670 --> 00:50:14.559
overall.

00:50:14.559 --> 00:50:21.289
So another example-- so this is an open API--
public API-- so anybody can connect their

00:50:21.289 --> 00:50:22.289
services.

00:50:22.289 --> 00:50:26.510
And the goal is to make it so that any kind
of service that supports research can be connected

00:50:26.510 --> 00:50:27.839
to the life cycle.

00:50:27.839 --> 00:50:33.589
So for example, the research computing center
at Notre Dame has a computational services

00:50:33.589 --> 00:50:37.670
engine that they use
to support their researchers.

00:50:37.670 --> 00:50:40.280
They collected that to the OSF.

00:50:40.280 --> 00:50:45.309
And by connecting it to the OSF-- then instead
of having to negotiate developing API relationships

00:50:45.309 --> 00:50:50.829
with each individual repository, once it was
connected to the OSF then Notre Dame users

00:50:50.829 --> 00:50:55.609
of that computational platform could apply
data that's stored in any environment that's

00:50:55.609 --> 00:50:57.150
already connected to the OSF.

00:50:57.150 --> 00:51:02.640
Whether it's on Dropbox, or Amazon S3 or their
own cloud service, or box.com, or otherwise.

00:51:02.640 --> 00:51:08.609
So the benefits can be moved rapidly to scale
by connecting particular services that support

00:51:08.609 --> 00:51:11.849
one's own area of research.

00:51:11.849 --> 00:51:16.740
The other key part of the OSF is that it is
a layered system.

00:51:16.740 --> 00:51:22.410
So that those core services at the base here--
the ecosystem of connecting services together,

00:51:22.410 --> 00:51:29.430
the data upon which it's all collected, and
then the services-authentication, security,

00:51:29.430 --> 00:51:31.810
management workflow, rendering in the browser.

00:51:31.810 --> 00:51:37.619
All those things are modularized in components
so that they can be put together in a variety

00:51:37.619 --> 00:51:42.430
of different ways with different interfaces
applied to them.

00:51:42.430 --> 00:51:47.740
So the OSF is one interface to access all
of those different services and all of that

00:51:47.740 --> 00:51:49.240
data and workflow management.

00:51:49.240 --> 00:51:54.450
But you can build any interface on top of
that core set of services because it's completely

00:51:54.450 --> 00:51:58.180
modularized from how the information is visualized.

00:51:58.180 --> 00:52:03.089
So one of those-- one example of that, just
to illustrate, is a generalized pre-print

00:52:03.089 --> 00:52:08.289
service that we have developed- and I'll show
you some images up in a second.

00:52:08.289 --> 00:52:09.740
And that's at the second layer.

00:52:09.740 --> 00:52:15.029
And then that service is modularized so that
it can be branded for any community to run

00:52:15.029 --> 00:52:16.940
their own version of that service.

00:52:16.940 --> 00:52:22.869
And so with the pre-print service, we have
10 or 11 now-- different groups that either

00:52:22.869 --> 00:52:27.820
have either launched or are soon to launch
their own pre-print service all built on the

00:52:27.820 --> 00:52:29.309
OSF.

00:52:29.309 --> 00:52:31.680
And done so very efficiently.

00:52:31.680 --> 00:52:37.670
And by this layering, we can enable scholarly
communities to focus on the areas of expertise

00:52:37.670 --> 00:52:38.750
that they have.

00:52:38.750 --> 00:52:46.850
So, for example, SOCARXIV, an open archive
for the social scientists, is run by sociologists.

00:52:46.850 --> 00:52:51.910
And they care very much about the content
of the research in the pre-prints and their

00:52:51.910 --> 00:52:55.069
research community that is using pre-prints.

00:52:55.069 --> 00:53:01.210
But they aren't experts and don't really care
about how security is managed in the service,

00:53:01.210 --> 00:53:04.010
[INAUDIBLE] and the kinds of databases are
used.

00:53:04.010 --> 00:53:08.580
That's left to the technical experts to care
about and focus their energies on.

00:53:08.580 --> 00:53:12.900
And then scholarly communication experts,
who think about information transmission and

00:53:12.900 --> 00:53:18.420
how pre-print services can get designed, focus
their efforts on designing that interface

00:53:18.420 --> 00:53:19.990
layer between them.

00:53:19.990 --> 00:53:24.220
And then the content area experts focus on
just delivery of the service and engaging

00:53:24.220 --> 00:53:26.400
their scholarly community.

00:53:26.400 --> 00:53:30.349
And so this really provides a great deal of
flexibility for the research community to

00:53:30.349 --> 00:53:36.109
be able to employ the same set of scholarly
tools-- scholarly commons-- for a lot of different

00:53:36.109 --> 00:53:37.109
uses.

00:53:37.109 --> 00:53:39.390
It can open up more of the research lifecycle.

00:53:39.390 --> 00:53:44.520
So pre-prints are a way to make research more
accessible to more people more quickly.

00:53:44.520 --> 00:53:50.799
And so if you go to OSF.io/preprints, you
can find the general search infrastructure.

00:53:50.799 --> 00:53:54.530
And this is an aggregated search across a
variety of different pre-print services, whether

00:53:54.530 --> 00:53:57.960
they're hosted by the Center for Open Science
or not.

00:53:57.960 --> 00:54:01.960
And you can search
against all of those in a single interface.

00:54:01.960 --> 00:54:09.880
And then simultaneously, each of those individual
services has their own URL and place for them

00:54:09.880 --> 00:54:13.630
to manage their own service, so that they
can run pre-prints on their own.

00:54:13.630 --> 00:54:17.330
So if anybody's interested in starting a pre-print
service, please do contact.

00:54:17.330 --> 00:54:23.520
We're happy to engage you on launching a pre-print
service for your community.

00:54:23.520 --> 00:54:27.240
And I'm going to close so that there's a couple
minutes for questions.

00:54:27.240 --> 00:54:33.680
But just to give an illustration of OSF institutions,
here's another view of that same set of core

00:54:33.680 --> 00:54:34.680
tools.

00:54:34.680 --> 00:54:39.400
I mentioned that Notre Dame Center for Research
Computing computational service connection

00:54:39.400 --> 00:54:40.410
as an example.

00:54:40.410 --> 00:54:47.940
But institutions can sign up for it OSF as
an institutional service-- it's a free service

00:54:47.940 --> 00:54:50.039
for institutions, too.

00:54:50.039 --> 00:54:55.819
And then make it easy for their researchers
to get onto the service-- single sign on capacity,

00:54:55.819 --> 00:54:58.490
so people can use their university credentials.

00:54:58.490 --> 00:55:02.289
And they can connect whatever services that
they have in their research environment, like

00:55:02.289 --> 00:55:07.950
a institutional repository, to the OSF to
make access of those services more efficient

00:55:07.950 --> 00:55:09.589
within their research community.

00:55:09.589 --> 00:55:15.270
There are a variety of different institutions
that are already using OSF institutions.

00:55:15.270 --> 00:55:17.220
This is just to illustrate.

00:55:17.220 --> 00:55:22.569
So let me close with that because there may
be some questions and there's a few minutes

00:55:22.569 --> 00:55:23.569
left.

00:55:23.569 --> 00:55:28.869
But just to point you to some links where
if you'd like to follow up with information

00:55:28.869 --> 00:55:30.059
to use any of these services.

00:55:30.059 --> 00:55:34.559
I didn't talk about training, but we have
free trainings that can be done remotely or

00:55:34.559 --> 00:55:38.160
in person on open or reproducible practices.

00:55:38.160 --> 00:55:40.380
And we are a service organization.

00:55:40.380 --> 00:55:44.329
So if you have a service that you use and
you want to connect to the OSF.

00:55:44.329 --> 00:55:50.160
If you have a community that you like to have--
make use of the OSF or get involved in the

00:55:50.160 --> 00:55:51.160
pre-reg challenge.

00:55:51.160 --> 00:55:55.950
Or if you're a journal editor-- you want to
pursue pre-registered reports as a pilot for

00:55:55.950 --> 00:55:56.950
your journal.

00:55:56.950 --> 00:56:00.420
Just feel free to get in touch with us for
any of these things.

00:56:00.420 --> 00:56:01.579
And here are links.

00:56:01.579 --> 00:56:03.609
And if you Google my name, I come up.

00:56:03.609 --> 00:56:05.460
There aren't many Nosek's in the world.

00:56:05.460 --> 00:56:06.750
So happy to chat.

00:56:06.750 --> 00:56:08.299
So thanks very much for your time and attention.

00:56:08.299 --> 00:56:10.049
I really appreciate it.

00:56:10.049 --> 00:56:13.519
Brian, thanks so much for this great talk.

00:56:13.519 --> 00:56:16.730
We do have, actually-- you got a lot of questions.

00:56:16.730 --> 00:56:21.460
I'm going to try and synthesize a few of them
together and maybe we can get through one

00:56:21.460 --> 00:56:22.599
or two of them.

00:56:22.599 --> 00:56:28.630
Obviously the badge system that you've proposed
here and described caught a lot of interest.

00:56:28.630 --> 00:56:33.140
And I think there's one or two questions around--
well actually, several questions around that.

00:56:33.140 --> 00:56:36.180
But maybe one or two T ones.

00:56:36.180 --> 00:56:43.970
One-- is it possible to apply the badges retrospectively
or retroactively to work that's already published

00:56:43.970 --> 00:56:48.839
and how far back could we go, if that was
possible?

00:56:48.839 --> 00:56:54.830
And secondly, how would abstract services
or indexing services-- like the National Library

00:56:54.830 --> 00:56:59.589
of Medicine's PubMed or Medline-- support
badges, do you think?

00:56:59.589 --> 00:57:05.990
Would it be possible, for instance, to add
something like that to their search mechanisms

00:57:05.990 --> 00:57:08.510
or their-- as a filter?

00:57:08.510 --> 00:57:10.880
So let's start with those two questions first.

00:57:10.880 --> 00:57:11.880
Yeah.

00:57:11.880 --> 00:57:12.880
Yeah, great question.

00:57:12.880 --> 00:57:17.480
And the answer is yes-- possible for both.

00:57:17.480 --> 00:57:19.079
And not active for both.

00:57:19.079 --> 00:57:22.040
So anybody can be
a badge issuer.

00:57:22.040 --> 00:57:26.819
And one of the things that we-- is in development,
it isn't in production yet, is a badge bakery.

00:57:26.819 --> 00:57:31.359
So that the badge itself has embedded in it
the metadata about who was the issuer, what

00:57:31.359 --> 00:57:33.010
was it issued for.

00:57:33.010 --> 00:57:37.880
You know, there is a DOI-- inserting the DOI,
et cetera.

00:57:37.880 --> 00:57:42.820
And that is to provide as much flexibility
as possible for these different kinds of uses--

00:57:42.820 --> 00:57:45.750
retrospective, prospective, different groups
applying badges.

00:57:45.750 --> 00:57:51.160
And then make them machine readable and recoverable
across a variety of different issuers.

00:57:51.160 --> 00:57:54.150
So yes, that's possible.

00:57:54.150 --> 00:58:00.309
And also, for the latter point, I'm getting
badges integrated with within NLM or other

00:58:00.309 --> 00:58:01.380
services.

00:58:01.380 --> 00:58:05.340
We're very interested in pursuing those kinds
of solutions.

00:58:05.340 --> 00:58:10.110
Because the real power of things like badges
are when they move to scale, right?

00:58:10.110 --> 00:58:13.030
It's great to show an example in a single
journal.

00:58:13.030 --> 00:58:18.140
What really the practice as a normative practice-and
especially getting integrated with things

00:58:18.140 --> 00:58:21.930
like one CB and other mechanisms of reward,
right?

00:58:21.930 --> 00:58:27.050
You can submit to your institution what portion
of your data is openly accessible for others

00:58:27.050 --> 00:58:30.849
as a mechanism of indicating good process.

00:58:30.849 --> 00:58:32.540
That will only work when it moves to scale.

00:58:32.540 --> 00:58:39.290
And so we're very interested in pursuing collaborations--
like with NLM and other groups for that.

00:58:39.290 --> 00:58:40.390
Great.

00:58:40.390 --> 00:58:43.710
So maybe one last question before we go.

00:58:43.710 --> 00:58:50.140
And this is more of a-- sort of a personal
question as a couple of people have asked

00:58:50.140 --> 00:58:51.140
this.

00:58:51.140 --> 00:58:57.369
But as a leader in this field, what was the
moment that sort of got you into open science?

00:58:57.369 --> 00:59:03.820
What was the light bulb or the point in time
which you sort of thought and saw that this

00:59:03.820 --> 00:59:06.390
was this was a key issue?

00:59:06.390 --> 00:59:12.190
Yeah I suspect that my experience was like
a lot of people's, where I didn't realize

00:59:12.190 --> 00:59:15.599
that science wasn't open when I started in
it.

00:59:15.599 --> 00:59:20.230
So it was only when I got into grad school
and I took a methodology class with Alan [? Casden

00:59:20.230 --> 00:59:21.750
?] my first year.

00:59:21.750 --> 00:59:26.109
Where I realized-- you know, we read all of
these papers in the 60s and 70s-- all these

00:59:26.109 --> 00:59:30.619
challenges of how science is done and what
the reality is.

00:59:30.619 --> 00:59:32.609
And I'm sort of stunned by that, right?

00:59:32.609 --> 00:59:35.670
I thought it was sort of like how we talked
about in second grade.

00:59:35.670 --> 00:59:37.180
You know, I'm curious about a question.

00:59:37.180 --> 00:59:40.740
And I study it and then I write down what
I did and I share that with the people.

00:59:40.740 --> 00:59:42.680
And then they say, oh what about this?

00:59:42.680 --> 00:59:44.150
What about that?

00:59:44.150 --> 00:59:48.519
And the fact that it doesn't operate like
that was sort of an awakening.

00:59:48.519 --> 00:59:53.869
My lab in grad school and in my lab-- since
I've been a faculty member-- I've always had

00:59:53.869 --> 00:59:55.440
a technical part of the lab.

00:59:55.440 --> 01:00:01.119
We've always just built tools to try to make
our research more efficient and more useful

01:00:01.119 --> 01:00:02.119
and more accessible.

01:00:02.119 --> 01:00:06.520
So I just sort of said, well we can make our
own data more available, even if we can't--

01:00:06.520 --> 01:00:09.260
even if the field isn't operating that way.

01:00:09.260 --> 01:00:14.670
So we've always had that voice-- and applying
for grants to try to increase-- build tools

01:00:14.670 --> 01:00:17.260
to make things more accessible.

01:00:17.260 --> 01:00:21.150
But the actual-- the development of the Center
for Open Science, which is a spin out from

01:00:21.150 --> 01:00:28.079
my lab, really was sort of a-- not a plan,
but sort of a grasping at an opportunity that

01:00:28.079 --> 01:00:29.079
sort of appeared.

01:00:29.079 --> 01:00:32.230
So I didn't talk about our reproducibility
projects.

01:00:32.230 --> 01:00:37.279
But we've done some large-scale replication
projects of studies-- in psychology first--

01:00:37.279 --> 01:00:38.279
my field.

01:00:38.279 --> 01:00:42.099
And we have one ongoing now in cancer biology.

01:00:42.099 --> 01:00:45.630
And we started to do that replication project
just as a lab

01:00:45.630 --> 01:00:49.359
project-- the one for psychology-- at the
same time that we were building sort of an

01:00:49.359 --> 01:00:51.859
initial version of the open science framework.

01:00:51.859 --> 01:00:53.710
And that got a lot of press attention.

01:00:53.710 --> 01:00:56.680
And then funders started calling.

01:00:56.680 --> 01:01:02.470
And our primary funder-- the Laura and John
Arnolds foundation-- gave us a $5.25 million

01:01:02.470 --> 01:01:04.710
award in order to start the center.

01:01:04.710 --> 01:01:10.339
And so what was sort of aspirations that were
realistic, of wouldn't it be great if we could

01:01:10.339 --> 01:01:12.170
build some tools that help a few people?

01:01:12.170 --> 01:01:16.529
Turned into, oh my gosh, we could really start
an organization and maybe really push on this.

01:01:16.529 --> 01:01:17.529
Let's go for it!

01:01:17.529 --> 01:01:23.750
And so that was-- you know, that gift just
really changed the direction of how we did

01:01:23.750 --> 01:01:24.750
this.

01:01:24.750 --> 01:01:25.750
OK.

01:01:25.750 --> 01:01:26.750
Well, we're at time.

01:01:26.750 --> 01:01:29.589
So it's 10 o'clock on the west coast.

01:01:29.589 --> 01:01:32.609
So again, Brian, thank you for your wonderful
insights on this.

01:01:32.609 --> 01:01:36.180
It was-- it was inspirational to hear a lot
of this work.

01:01:36.180 --> 01:01:37.829
And we'll end the session here.

01:01:37.829 --> 01:01:39.260
Thanks very much.

01:01:39.260 --> 01:01:41.170
I appreciate everybody attending.

